# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for the stdcxx revival
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil ^:nil
#+TODO: TODO WAITING | DONE

The queue, one chapter per part of the tree, in the order the work is
wanted; within a chapter, priority order top first. Each entry states
the fact, the fix and the check that settles it. A =WAITING= entry is
investigated and reported and waits on its owner; it leaves when the
fix it waits for lands and its check passes. Finished entries leave;
git keeps them. The =analysis-= notes under =doc/notes= carry the
longer investigations.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* Library

The library itself, =include= and =src=: its behaviour, its interface
and what it carries that it no longer uses. The entries are binned by
the clause of the standard they fall under, in clause order, and the
tree's own housekeeping comes last. Within a bin the easiest entry is
at the top.

* Tests

The suite and its driver under =tests=: what it covers, what it
misses, and how it runs.

** TODO Three tests guard their =wchar_t= run with a macro nothing defines

=tests/localization/22.locale.numpunct.cpp= 424,
=tests/iostream/27.ios.members.static.cpp= 235 and 274, and
=tests/include/rw_char.h= 484 wrap their =wchar_t= code in
~#ifndef _RWSTD_WCHAR_T~ (222c045b, 2008). Nothing in the tree
defines =_RWSTD_WCHAR_T=, and no =_RWSTD_NO_WCHAR_T= exists either, so
the guards are always true: dead conditionals around code that always
compiles. Found 2026-11-07.

Fix: the three guards go, the code they wrap stays.

Check: =grep -rn _RWSTD_WCHAR_T tests= finds nothing; the three tests
hold at pin.

** TODO The header-guard message in =tests/src/new.cpp= reads past its arguments

The lead-guard check's =rw_error= has the format ~"%s:%d: header
guard corruption at %#p: expected %#p, got %#p"~ and the arguments
=hdr->ptr_, (const void*)hdr, hdr->self_=: =__FILE__= and
=__LINE__= are missing, so =%s= reads the user block as a string and
the last two =%#p= read arguments that are not there. The check
moved from =_rw_find_block= into =_rw_check_block= as it was. Seen
2026-11-05.

Fix: =__FILE__, __LINE__,= before =hdr->ptr_=.

Check: a =0.new= case that corrupts =self_= of a live block and then
calls =rwt_check_leaks (0, 0)= prints the file and line.

** TODO =_rw_find_block='s =check_heap= parameter is always true

Since =operator_delete= checks the block in hand, every caller of
=_rw_find_block= (=tests/src/new.cpp=) passes =true=; the =false=
path, which returned the block on the first match, has no caller.
Seen 2026-11-05.

Fix: drop the parameter and the branch, or keep them as interface.

Check: a sanity build of the test driver.

** TODO =operator_delete= reads the header before any pointer it is given

The in-hand check reads 48 bytes before the pointer before it knows
the pointer is a live block. On a freed block that is a read of
freed memory: harmless under glibc for =0.new='s double delete
(=self_= reads as the fill), a fault for an =mmap=-served block that
=malloc= has unmapped (none in the suite), and a heap-use-after-free
report under AddressSanitizer, where the old walk never touched freed
memory. Hypothesis, 2026-11-05.

Fix: a decision after the next sanitizer run of =0.new=: accept the
report in the self-test, or keep a side table of live pointers.

Check: =0.new= under 15D-asan.

** TODO Two ctype test defects: a wrong character and an unbraced =TEST=

=tests/localization/22.locale.ctype.is.cpp= line 822 tests
=ct.widen ('\xa0')= for =DIGIT | GRAPH | PRINT= where
=22.locale.ctype.cpp= tests ='\xa2'=; the row passes only because =is=
matches any one bit. =22.locale.ctype.tolower.cpp= line 262 and
=22.locale.ctype.toupper.cpp= line 265 write ~if (sizeof (charT) > 1)
TEST (...)~ without braces, and =TEST= expands to two statements, so
the char pass asserts a stale =success=. Seen while adding the wide
rows beside them.

Fix: ='\xa2'= in the =is= row; braces around the two =TEST= calls.

Check: the =is= row stays green at the same count; =tolower= and
=toupper= each lose one assertion in the char pass; the eight pins take
the new totals.

** TODO A test that generates a locale ends on a driver warning

A test that builds a locale with =rw_localedef= prints
~driver.cpp:1392: rw_note(): warning: test finished, cannot call~ as
it exits. =atexit_rm_locale_root= (=tests/src/locale.cpp=) disables
notes and calls =rw_system=, whose =_rw_vsystem=
(=tests/src/process.cpp= 158) calls =rw_note=; =_rw_check_init=
warns that the driver is done before the disabled note is dropped.
Seen in =22.locale.time.get= and =22.locale.time.put=, 2026-11-04.

Fix: the removal without =rw_system='s note, or =_rw_check_init=
silent for a disabled diagnostic; a decision.

Check: both tests end without the line; the pins unchanged.

** TODO The suite against other libraries

The suite tests the C++03 library through clauses 18 to 27. Most of
its assertions check standard behaviour, not the library's additions.
Run against libstdc++ and libc++, a disagreement is a defect in one of
the three or a question the standard leaves open.

Fix: build the driver and the tests of clauses 21 to 27 against
another library. The tests that need the library's additions or its
=_RWSTD_= macros are listed and left out, each with the reason.

Check: the census of the tests that build, a table per library, and
each disagreement read against the standard and filed where it
belongs.

** WAITING ThreadSanitizer cannot follow =longjmp= on glibc 2.44

Under the sanitizer every =longjmp= and =siglongjmp= dies with "can't
find longjmp buf" and exit 66. A ten-line program with =sigsetjmp=
and =siglongjmp= and no signal fails the same way, under GCC 16's
libtsan and Clang 23's runtime alike. The sanitizer records the stack
pointer at =setjmp= and, at =longjmp=, demangles the one saved in the
=jmp_buf= to find the record. glibc 2.44 (a5ec880f80, "Consolidate
pointer guard to use a relro variable instead of the TCB") moved the
x86-64 pointer guard from the thread control block slot at =%fs:0x30=
to the variable =__pointer_chk_guard=; the slot now reads zero, and
the runtimes still demangle with it. The same series gave aarch64 a
rotate its runtime does not undo (78f1f0e39c). Reported upstream as
LLVM issue 228263 (2026-10); GCC's libsanitizer takes the fix at its
next merge from compiler-rt. The six programs of the spot-check note
are the ones this stops.

Fix: the sanitizer runtimes'. The tests need no change.

Check: =0.alloc=, =0.new=, =0.inputiter=, =0.outputiter=,
=18.setjmp= and =18.csetjmp= exit 0 under a sanitizer runtime that
reads the new guard.

