** TODO A failed output row of =27.ostream= prints the wrong strings

=tests/iostream/27.ostream.cpp= 750-754, in =test_formatted=, format
a failed comparison as ~operator<<(%s = %{#lc}) inserted "%s",
expected "%s"~ but pass =line2=, =cname=, =tname=, =cbuf= and =str=,
no =val=. So =%{#lc}= takes =cbuf=, the inserted field prints the
expected string and the expected field reads past the arguments: a
row that expects "-1" and gets "65535" reports that it inserted "-1"
and expected garbage. The value goes through =%{#lc}=, a character
directive, in this assertion's siblings too, for every type including
=double=. Seen while adding the =basefield= rows of the =short= and
=int= inserters, 2026-11-06.

Fix: pass =val= before =cbuf=; format the value with a directive of
its type.

Check: a row made to fail prints the value, the inserted string and
the expected one.

** TODO =27.ostream='s =test_streambuf= leaves =pstrm_= unset in its buffer constructor

=tests/iostream/27.ostream.cpp= 73-77: the constructor that takes a
buffer and its size initializes every member but =pstrm_=, and =sync=
(138) calls =setstate= through =pstrm_= when it is not null. A row that
reaches =sync= on such a buffer thus reads an indeterminate pointer.
None does today: =test_num_put::test= (347) and =test_formatted= (913)
never call =sync=, and the third user (1670) sets =pstrm_= to 0 itself.
=fail_when_= (154) is static, so =test_formatted= (930) also leaves it
changed for every later =test_streambuf= of the character type; the
=test_sentry= rows that fail or throw on the first =sync= hold only
because =test_sentry= runs first. Seen while adding the tie rows of
=test_sentry=, 2026-11-09.

Fix: the constructor sets =pstrm_= to 0; =fail_when_= becomes a member
that each constructor sets to 1.

Check: reading; the test at its pin.

** TODO =cformat::do_out= stores characters and returns =noconv=

The test facet =cformat= in =tests/iostream/27.filebuf.codecvt.cpp=
copies each character that needs no escape to the destination (357).
When no character of the sequence needed one it returns =noconv= with
the next pointers reset (361-365), and the copies stay stored.
14882:2003 22.2.1.5.2, p2 asks of a =noconv= result that "there are
no changes to the values in [to, to_limit)". =basic_filebuf= writes
the source sequence on =noconv=, so no row sees the stored
characters. By reading, 2026-11-13.

Fix: =do_out= looks for a character to escape before it stores any.

Check: a =self_test= row calls =out= on a sequence without escapes
over a destination filled with a sentinel; the =noconv= result leaves
the sentinel in place.

** TODO =cformat::do_unshift= leaves =to_next= unset

=cformat::do_unshift= in =tests/iostream/27.filebuf.codecvt.cpp=
(113-118) returns =noconv= and assigns nothing to =to_next=.
14882:2003 22.2.1.5.2, p5: "It always leaves the to_next pointer
pointing one beyond the last element successfully stored", which is
=to= where nothing is stored. By reading, 2026-11-13.

Fix: the function sets =to_next= to =to=.

Check: a =self_test= row calls =unshift= with =to_next= null and
finds it equal to =to=.

