** TODO The collate dump of =locale= shows neither directions nor multibyte keys

=locale -ck LC_COLLATE= prints one =order_start= line for a database,
from the header's =weight_type= (=util/locale.cpp= 1060-1075), which
holds one section's directions, and prints the weights of each element
without the direction word stored in front of them (=print_weight=,
the FIXME at 861 skips the word); for the =22.locale.collate= locale,
whose first section is ~forward;backward;forward,position~ and whose
last is all forward, the dump says ~forward;forward;forward~. It also
prints a key of more than one byte by its first byte: =<U00E8>= comes
out as ~\d195~. The dump thus cannot show whether an element is
=forward= or =forward,position=, which decided a pair of that test
locale on 2026-11-08.

Fix: print each section's directions from the words the elements
carry, and decode a key of more than one byte through the charmap
before printing it (=write_coll_info= builds the key byte by byte).

Check: the dump of the =22.locale.collate= locale shows
=forward;backward;forward,position= for its first section and
=<U00E8>= by name.

** TODO The =localedef= objects have no header dependencies

